1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,853
South Korea's captain just became the country's all-time top scorer.

2
00:00:06,133 --> 00:00:12,751
And according to a claim made in parliament last week, some of his own teammates don't even call him by his name.

3
00:00:13,031 --> 00:00:15,178
They call him number seven.

4
00:00:15,908 --> 00:00:22,175
A single Korean news clip about it passed two point six million views in less than a week.

5
00:00:22,455 --> 00:00:24,996
So what are Koreans actually saying?

6
00:00:25,726 --> 00:00:32,006
Today we'll go through the claim itself, the comments with thousands of likes, and the people who see it differently.

7
00:00:32,286 --> 00:00:37,823
And at the end, one comment that sums up how a lot of Korea feels right now.

8
00:00:38,553 --> 00:00:40,930
First, what actually happened.

9
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:49,748
On October twenty-first, during the National Assembly's annual audit of the Korea Football Association, a lawmaker raised a claim.

10
00:00:50,028 --> 00:00:58,140
Some players on the national team, the lawmaker said, refer to captain Son Heung-min as number seven,

11
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:02,155
or simply that person, instead of saying his name.

12
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:09,678
When asked, the federation said it had received reports that players were struggling with problems inside the team.

13
00:01:09,958 --> 00:01:17,635
It did not confirm the nickname itself, and said that what happens in the locker room should be solved in the locker room.

14
00:01:18,365 --> 00:01:22,015
Within days, the story had spread beyond Korea.

15
00:01:22,295 --> 00:01:28,147
Japanese outlets reported that the turmoil inside the Korean team would likely continue,

16
00:01:28,147 --> 00:01:34,265
and Korean broadcasters ran follow-up segments about how the claim was being covered abroad.

17
00:01:34,545 --> 00:01:40,111
For a player as famous as Son, a story like this was never going to stay local.

18
00:01:40,841 --> 00:01:45,689
To understand why this hit so hard in Korea, you need some context.

19
00:01:45,969 --> 00:01:52,843
On October sixth, against Uzbekistan, Son curled a free kick into the bottom corner in the sixth minute.

20
00:01:53,123 --> 00:01:59,856
It was his fifty-ninth goal for Korea. It broke the national record held by Cha Bum-kun,

21
00:01:59,856 --> 00:02:06,895
a name every Korean football fan knows, a record that had stood since the nineteen eighties.

22
00:02:07,175 --> 00:02:13,613
In England, Tottenham's official account congratulated him as a Spurs legend. In Los Angeles,

23
00:02:13,613 --> 00:02:16,452
his club LAFC posted a message in Korean.

24
00:02:16,732 --> 00:02:24,654
So for Koreans, the timing felt brutal. The week he made history, the story became about what his own teammates call him.

25
00:02:25,384 --> 00:02:32,254
Around the same time, Korean media reported that three key players born in nineteen ninety-six,

26
00:02:32,254 --> 00:02:38,544
Kim Min-jae, Hwang In-beom and Hwang Hee-chan, had hinted at leaving the national team.

27
00:02:38,824 --> 00:02:42,236
Korean fans call them the ninety-six line.

28
00:02:42,516 --> 00:02:46,802
None of the three has publicly confirmed any conflict with Son.

29
00:02:47,532 --> 00:02:49,553
And this isn't the first time.

30
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:55,963
Back in January twenty twenty-four, on the night before the Asian Cup semifinal,

31
00:02:55,963 --> 00:03:05,770
Son clashed with younger players who wanted to play table tennis after dinner. He dislocated a finger. Lee Kang-in later flew to

32
00:03:05,770 --> 00:03:10,367
London to apologize in person, and Son publicly forgave him.

33
00:03:10,647 --> 00:03:18,438
During this summer's World Cup, Korean outlets reported that the team boycotted interviews after criticism of Son,

34
00:03:18,438 --> 00:03:21,513
and that not every player was happy about it.

35
00:03:21,793 --> 00:03:28,721
So when the number seven claim came out, many Koreans didn't see a single incident. They saw a pattern.

36
00:03:29,451 --> 00:03:34,779
Second. The first wave of reactions was not anger. It was sympathy.

37
00:03:35,509 --> 00:03:39,800
Under the JTBC news clip, one of the most liked comments reads:

38
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:46,110
Oh, Heung-min. How hard must it have been all this time. My heart hurts.

39
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:55,934
Another, with over two thousand likes, calls him a saint, a word Koreans use for someone with almost unreal patience.

40
00:03:56,214 --> 00:03:59,885
And then it adds: now I'm scared he'll retire.

41
00:04:00,615 --> 00:04:03,773
The most common feeling was simple disbelief.

42
00:04:04,053 --> 00:04:10,190
Number seven? Zero respect for a legend. That one has three thousand likes.

43
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:14,097
Under a pundit interview, a shorter one says it all.

44
00:04:14,377 --> 00:04:18,308
Just leave Heung-min alone. He's on a completely different level.

45
00:04:19,038 --> 00:04:28,104
And a theme that came up again and again: what amazes people is not the claim itself, but that he kept quiet through all of it.

46
00:04:28,384 --> 00:04:32,543
What amazes me more is that he put up with this until now.

47
00:04:33,273 --> 00:04:35,998
Third. This is where it gets interesting.

48
00:04:36,278 --> 00:04:40,902
Many Koreans started looking back at matches they had already watched.

49
00:04:41,632 --> 00:04:45,825
One viewer wrote that Son always seemed gloomy with the national team,

50
00:04:45,825 --> 00:04:48,880
and bright again whenever he went back to his club.

51
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:52,522
So I wasn't the only one who noticed.

52
00:04:53,252 --> 00:04:55,834
Under a different report, another wrote:

53
00:04:56,114 --> 00:05:02,219
I kept feeling like the ball just wasn't being passed to him. I thought I was being paranoid.

54
00:05:02,949 --> 00:05:05,941
And the top comment there connects it to results.

55
00:05:06,221 --> 00:05:11,374
So there was a reason the team kept playing so badly. No wonder there was no teamwork.

56
00:05:12,104 --> 00:05:17,189
Fourth. A lot of the anger was not aimed at the players at all.

57
00:05:17,919 --> 00:05:23,823
There's a Korean saying that the water downstream is only as clean as the water upstream.

58
00:05:24,103 --> 00:05:31,433
Hundreds of viewers used it to blame the federation, which has faced years of criticism over how it runs the national team.

59
00:05:32,163 --> 00:05:36,262
Fifth. Something that might surprise viewers outside Korea.

60
00:05:36,542 --> 00:05:41,569
For a lot of fans, Son is the reason they watch the national team at all.

61
00:05:42,299 --> 00:05:49,764
If it weren't for Son, I wouldn't even care about the national team. Get it together. That's over a thousand likes.

62
00:05:50,494 --> 00:05:56,959
And another: when Son retires, I'll just stop watching. It's only a ball game anyway.

63
00:05:57,239 --> 00:06:02,883
Comments like these were everywhere. Shorter versions too. No Sonny, no football.

64
00:06:03,613 --> 00:06:07,906
That attachment is also why the numbers are so big.

65
00:06:08,186 --> 00:06:15,185
An MBC news recap about the retirement reports alone has passed three point four million views.

66
00:06:15,915 --> 00:06:21,886
But not everyone in Korea sees this as a simple story of a captain being bullied.

67
00:06:22,616 --> 00:06:29,973
Well-known commentator Park Moon-sung pointed out that conflict exists in every team, in every country.

68
00:06:30,253 --> 00:06:38,920
In Europe, he said, these stories usually come out years later, in autobiographies. The real problem is that this one became public

69
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,897
while the players are still playing together.

70
00:06:42,177 --> 00:06:50,189
His suggestion: the people involved should say clearly where they stand, instead of letting rumors do the talking.

71
00:06:50,919 --> 00:06:56,414
Other voices in Korean media argued that a generational change is overdue.

72
00:06:56,694 --> 00:07:03,443
One club coach even suggested that Son should step down as captain and support the younger players.

73
00:07:03,723 --> 00:07:11,039
And reports have also pointed to friction going back years, including an interview boycott during the World Cup.

74
00:07:11,769 --> 00:07:13,434
So what happens now?

75
00:07:14,164 --> 00:07:20,534
Interim coach Roberto Moreno announces his November squad on October twenty-ninth.

76
00:07:20,814 --> 00:07:28,104
Korea then plays Saudi Arabia on November thirteenth and Qatar on November seventeenth, both in Doha.

77
00:07:28,384 --> 00:07:36,701
Korean media say the big question is simple. Does he call up both Son and the ninety-six line, and try to make it work?

78
00:07:37,431 --> 00:07:38,914
So, to sum up.

79
00:07:39,194 --> 00:07:44,450
A claim in parliament that some players call their captain number seven.

80
00:07:44,730 --> 00:07:47,563
A wave of sympathy, then anger.

81
00:07:47,843 --> 00:07:52,083
Fans rewatching old matches and wondering what they missed.

82
00:07:52,363 --> 00:07:54,377
Plenty of blame for the federation.

83
00:07:54,657 --> 00:08:00,198
And a smaller group saying conflict is normal, and change is overdue.

84
00:08:00,928 --> 00:08:03,349
And here is the comment I promised.

85
00:08:03,629 --> 00:08:09,393
Don't even say the word retire about Son. Not now. Absolutely not.

86
00:08:10,123 --> 00:08:12,680
That's what Korea is saying this week.

87
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:19,336
If you want to know what Koreans think about the next big story, subscribe. We'll translate it for you.
